# Singly Linked List — Basics

## 1. Node Structure

A Linked List consists of nodes.

Each node contains:
- data → stores the value
- next → reference to the next node

class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

## 2. Traversal

Start from head and move using next until null.

Node temp = head;

while (temp != null) {
    System.out.println(temp.data);
    temp = temp.next;
}

## 3. Insertion at Head

Create a new node, point it to the current head, then make it the new head.

Node newNode = new Node(value);
newNode.next = head;
head = newNode;

Example:
Before: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> null
After inserting 5: 5 -> 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> null

Time: O(1)

## 4. Deletion of Head

Move head to the next node.

if (head != null) {
    head = head.next;
}

Example:
Before: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> null
After: 20 -> 30 -> null

Time: O(1)

## 5. Find Length

Traverse the entire list and count the nodes.

int count = 0;
Node temp = head;

while (temp != null) {
    count++;
    temp = temp.next;
}

return count;

Time: O(n)

## 6. Search in Linked List

Traverse the list and check each node.

Node temp = head;

while (temp != null) {
    if (temp.data == target) {
        return true;
    }
    temp = temp.next;
}

return false;

Time: O(n)

## Key Points

head = first node
next = reference to next node
last node's next = null

Basic traversal pattern:

Node temp = head;

while (temp != null) {
    // process temp.data
    temp = temp.next;
}

Operations:
- Insert at Head → O(1)
- Delete Head → O(1)
- Find Length → O(n)
- Search → O(n)
- Traversal → O(n)